<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Inclusive Design on Rizzykun</title><link>https://rizzykun.github.io/tags/inclusive-design/</link><description>Recent content in Inclusive Design on Rizzykun</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Tue, 16 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://rizzykun.github.io/tags/inclusive-design/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Accessible STEM Laboratory</title><link>https://rizzykun.github.io/projects/accessible-stem-lab/</link><pubDate>Tue, 16 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://rizzykun.github.io/projects/accessible-stem-lab/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://rizzykun.github.io/projects/accessible-stem-lab/project__usrah-in-action-stem-lab__12.jpg" alt="Featured image of post Accessible STEM Laboratory" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;An eight-month project through &lt;strong&gt;Usrah in Action&lt;/strong&gt;, a subject run by the
Sejahtera Centre of IIUM, where I served as &lt;strong&gt;Project Manager&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="the-problem"&gt;&lt;a href="#the-problem" class="header-anchor"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;The problem
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Our team proposed a STEM laboratory design for a school serving visually
impaired students. The question we kept coming back to: how do you make STEM
subjects engaging for students who need a fundamentally different learning
environment?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id="the-sensory-wall"&gt;&lt;a href="#the-sensory-wall" class="header-anchor"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;The sensory wall
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;The centrepiece of the proposed lab is a &lt;strong&gt;sensory wall&lt;/strong&gt; — students explore
mechanical, electronic and STEM tools through touch, audio guidance and
Braille, rather than through a screen or a whiteboard.&lt;/p&gt;
